course: dictionary
lesson: 12722
title: Downtown: the city centre
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: "Downtown" is a word used mostly in the United States and Canada. It means the main business and shopping area in the centre of a city or town. «We took the bus downtown to see the lights.» Here, downtown means into the busy centre of the city.
explain: The words around a word give clues. In «The downtown area was full of tall offices and busy shops», the clues are tall offices and busy shops. So downtown must be the busy centre of a city, not a quiet village.
explain: Downtown can be a noun, an adverb or an adjective. As a noun it names the place: «the downtown». As an adverb it tells you where: «go downtown». As an adjective it describes a place: «downtown Chicago».
explain: People in Britain and Ireland often say "the town centre" or "in town". Australians often say "the city" or "the CBD". If you read that someone is "heading downtown", picture the busy centre.
fact: Downtown means the main business and shopping area in the centre of a city or town.
fact: «Downtown» is mostly used in the United States and Canada.
fact: «Go downtown» means to travel into the city centre.
fact: «Downtown Chicago» means the central part of Chicago.
fact: British speakers often say "the town centre" or "in town" instead.
exercise: «We took the bus downtown to see the lights.» Where did they go? | Under the sea | *To the busy centre of the city | To the top of a mountain | A farm in the countryside
exercise: What does downtown mean in «The downtown area was full of tall offices and busy shops»? | *The main business and shopping area in the centre | A quiet village by the river | A small field full of sheep | A place in the far north of a country
exercise: In «The downtown area was full of tall offices and busy shops», which word is a clue that downtown is a busy place? | *offices | full | was | area
test: «They walked downtown after school.» What kind of word is downtown here? | adjective | noun | verb | *adverb
test: A friend in Toronto says, «I'm going downtown.» What does she mean? | To a farm | To the countryside | *To the city centre | To the seaside
exam: Which sentence uses downtown as an adjective? | Downtown is where I was born. | We walked downtown this morning. | Let's go downtown. | *Our downtown hotel is near the stadium.
